UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 36, Lesson 1: Choosing a research question', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Choosing a research question means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to investigate with available sources.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort better focus: (1) oceans (2) Why do rock pools need careful stepping?\n\nFEEDBACK\n(2) is a manageable how/why question; (1) is too broad without narrowing.\n\nCHECK\nA question with no available sources will stall the project.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nList five interests, narrow each to a how/why question, rate source access and choose one research question for the unit.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Choosing a research question means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to investigate with available sources.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: List five interests, narrow each to a how/why question, rate source access and choose one research question for the unit. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using research on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A question with no available sources will stall the project.. Correction: (2) is a manageable how/why question;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A question with no available sources will stall the project. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating research on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with research, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about research with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for research. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for research. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","(2) is a manageable how/why question","A question with no available sources will stall the project","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Sort better focus: (1) oceans (2) Why do rock pools need careful stepping?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for research; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choosing a research question means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to investigate","(2) is a manageable how/why question","Careful use of question and explain why it matters with clear evidence","A question with no available sources will stall the project"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with research?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named question and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A question with no available sources will stall the project","Name question and explain why it matters only, without linking it to research","Apply research to new material and name the question and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies research with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries research. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words","A question with no available sources will stall the project","Point to exact words in the sentence that show research and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event"],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses research from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name question and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","(2) is a manageable how/why question","A question with no available sources will stall the project","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about research?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse question and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for research?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about research","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","A question with no available sources will stall the project"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of research on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about research with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating research on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using research on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for research. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with research, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: A question with no available sources will stall the project.. Correction: (2) is a manageable how/why question;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w36-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise research.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use research with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify question and explain why it matters.","I can practise focus independently on a new prompt.","I can notice sources needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Choosing a research question means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to investigate with available sources.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation","Choosing a research question means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to investigate"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about research with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"How do honeybees share flower direction?\" beats \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + kid-friendly sources.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort better focus: (1) oceans (2) Why do rock pools need careful stepping?\n\nFEEDBACK\n(2) is a manageable how/why question; (1) is too broad without narrowing.","explain":"Choosing a research question means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to investigate with available sources.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A question with no available sources will stall the project. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Research question focus test.","caption":"Interest must meet sources.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Focused</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How bees share direction</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Too broad</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">everything about insects</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"How do honeybees share flower direction?\" beats \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + kid-friendly sources. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"How do honeybees share flower direction?\" beats \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + kid-friendly sources. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about research with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"How do honeybees share flower direction?\" beats \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + kid-friendly sources. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Manageable questions vs source-less topics.","caption":"Choose one unit question.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Narrow</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">why/how question</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stall risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no sources available</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about research with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA question with no available sources will stall the project. Here is a more productive model: \"How do honeybees share flower direction?\" beats \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + kid-friendly sources.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\n(2) is a manageable how/why question; (1) is too broad without narrowing.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. A question with no available sources will stall the project. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about research with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChoosing a research question means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to investigate with available sources. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 36, Lesson 1: Choosing a research question","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Independent research and portfolio","week":36,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"How do honeybees share flower direction?\" beats \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + kid-friendly sources.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Research question focus test.","caption":"Interest must meet sources.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Focused</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How bees share direction</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Too broad</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">everything about insects</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Manageable questions vs source-less topics.","caption":"Choose one unit question.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Narrow</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">why/how question</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stall risk</text